.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause
.Dd September 30, 2026
.Dt CRONTAB 1
.Os OxideBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm crontab
.Nd install, list, edit or remove a user's table of scheduled commands
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl u Ar user
.Ar file
.Nm
.Op Fl u Ar user
.Fl l | e
.Nm
.Op Fl u Ar user
.Fl r Op Fl f
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
manages the table of commands
.Xr cron 8
runs for a user, in the format of
.Xr crontab 5 .
.Pp
Given a
.Ar file ,
or
.Ql -
for the standard input,
.Nm
installs it as the user's table, replacing any other.
.Pp
The options are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl e
Edit the table: a copy is opened with the editor named by
.Ev VISUAL ,
or else
.Ev EDITOR ,
or else
.Xr vi 1 ,
and installed when the editor exits, if it changed.
An edit that isn't a valid table is not installed: the errors are shown, and
.Nm
offers to edit it again.
.It Fl f
With
.Fl r ,
don't ask first.
.It Fl l
Write the table to the standard output.
.It Fl r
Remove the table, after asking, unless
.Fl f .
.It Fl u Ar user
Act on the table of
.Ar user
instead of one's own.
Only root may.
.El
.Pp
A table is checked before it is installed: one with an error is refused, and
each error is reported with its line number, so that a mistake never replaces
a working table.
Tables are kept in
.Pa /var/cron/tabs ,
owned by root and readable only by root, and
.Xr cron 8
reads a new one within a minute.
.Pp
Root may always use
.Nm .
Other users may if
.Pa /var/cron/allow
exists and lists them, one name per line; if it doesn't exist, every user may
whom
.Pa /var/cron/deny
doesn't list.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width /var/cron/tabs/user -compact
.It Pa /var/cron/tabs/ Ns Ar user
the user's table
.It Pa /var/cron/allow
users who may use
.Nm
.It Pa /var/cron/deny
users who may not
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr crontab 5 ,
.Xr cron 8
.Sh HISTORY
A
.Nm
command appeared in
.At V .
.Sh BUGS
On the BSDs
.Nm
is set-user-ID root, which lets every user write their table to
.Pa /var/cron/tabs .
OxideBSD doesn't run set-user-ID programs yet, so for now only root can use
.Nm ;
other users are told so.
